WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.004
The top number is every triangle in this scene.

00:00:03.004 --> 00:00:05.558
The yellow one is how many actually get drawn.

00:00:05.938 --> 00:00:09.218
Each colour is a cluster of about a hundred and twenty-eight triangles,

00:00:09.218 --> 00:00:11.132
and each one picks its own detail.

00:00:13.835 --> 00:00:14.765
Hi, I'm Vertie.

00:00:14.765 --> 00:00:15.695
This is three-nanite.

00:00:15.695 --> 00:00:17.866
Let's build its hierarchy, then cut it.

00:00:21.867 --> 00:00:24.084
Take a few neighbouring clusters as a group.

00:00:24.084 --> 00:00:26.579
The vertices it shares with other groups are locked.

00:00:26.959 --> 00:00:28.832
Merge them and simplify to half.

00:00:28.832 --> 00:00:32.267
The outer border cannot move, so it still meets its neighbours.

00:00:32.647 --> 00:00:36.874
Split the result into half as many clusters, and repeat until one is left.

00:00:38.825 --> 00:00:42.200
Here is a small hierarchy, the source clusters at the bottom, each

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:43.606
row half the one below.

00:00:44.026 --> 00:00:47.597
A cluster is drawn when its error, projected to pixels, is small

00:00:47.597 --> 00:00:49.382
enough, and its parent's is not.

00:00:49.802 --> 00:00:52.011
Move the camera, and the cut follows it.

00:00:52.011 --> 00:00:54.497
Fine where the camera is close, coarse far away.

00:00:55.802 --> 00:00:59.715
Now let each child measure its parent from its own bounds.

00:00:59.715 --> 00:01:01.493
Siblings disagree, and holes open.

00:01:03.358 --> 00:01:05.556
Every group carries one error and one sphere.

00:01:05.976 --> 00:01:08.993
The group's error in mesh units, times a constant from the screen

00:01:08.993 --> 00:01:10.250
height and field of view.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.961
Divided by the distance to the nearest point of its sphere.

00:01:13.961 --> 00:01:16.055
Siblings share that sphere, so they agree.

00:01:17.825 --> 00:01:19.892
The article's widget does the same thing.

00:01:19.892 --> 00:01:22.253
With the shared test, the curve never breaks.

00:01:22.633 --> 00:01:25.369
Switch to own bounds, and three holes open at once.

00:01:25.749 --> 00:01:27.231
Back to shared, and they close.

00:01:28.783 --> 00:01:31.453
So I built the whole hierarchy in the page.

00:01:31.453 --> 00:01:33.529
Twenty-five rocks, each choosing its own cut.

00:01:33.529 --> 00:01:35.012
The colour is the level.

00:01:35.392 --> 00:01:39.223
Raise the threshold, and every rock climbs to coarser levels, until only

00:01:39.223 --> 00:01:41.457
the top of the hierarchy is left.

00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:44.511
Thirty-two million triangles in the scene.

00:01:44.511 --> 00:01:48.600
At one pixel, the cut draws about two hundred and twenty-eight thousand.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.007
My crack test found no open edges in any cut.

00:01:53.575 --> 00:01:56.327
Zoom in, and the clusters near the camera get small.

00:01:56.707 --> 00:01:58.296
Freeze the cut and pull back.

00:01:58.296 --> 00:02:00.945
Only what was inside the old view was ever drawn.

00:02:02.392 --> 00:02:05.294
What makes Nanite fast is the other half.

00:02:05.294 --> 00:02:09.284
Clusters of triangles under thirty-two pixels go to a compute rasteriser.

00:02:09.684 --> 00:02:14.010
It writes depth and ids with one atomic, and shades each pixel once, afterwards.

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:16.422
Nanite streams pages of groups.

00:02:16.422 --> 00:02:18.031
three-nanite streams whole levels.

00:02:18.431 --> 00:02:21.418
And it culls hidden clusters, not just hidden props.

00:02:21.418 --> 00:02:25.068
WebGL2 has no compute shaders, so none of this is there.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.612
So the win is drawing fewer triangles, not cheaper ones.

00:02:29.612 --> 00:02:32.142
Here the yellow number stays under two million.

00:02:33.308 --> 00:02:36.437
The hierarchy and its cut work in WebGL2.

00:02:36.437 --> 00:02:38.783
Cheap pixel-sized triangles still need compute.

00:02:40.275 --> 00:02:41.114
Group, lock, halve.

00:02:41.114 --> 00:02:41.953
Share the bounds.

00:02:41.953 --> 00:02:43.910
And try the demo in the article.

00:02:44.875 --> 00:02:46.924
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:46.924 --> 00:02:47.510
I'm Vertie.

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:47.803
Bye!
